AI-GENERATED CODE

All source code in this repository was generated by an AI model: Claude
(made by Anthropic; model "Claude Fable 5.2", used through Claude Code),
working under the direction of Pearce Harshbarger. Every source file
carries a header that says so. AI was also used to research public
documentation and to draft the text of the documentation and submission.

The concept and its five ideas are the team owner's own, written in their
design note "Tombaugh: a blink comparator for the 102-color sky"
(13 November 2026). The AI was not the source of the idea.

The repository contains no AI-generated images or video. The preview
animations on the landing page are drawn procedurally by code in the
browser. See docs/AI-USE.md for the full log.


THIRD-PARTY CODE

None. No third-party code libraries are bundled or imported at run time;
there are no npm dependencies and no CDN imports. All code in the
repository was generated for this project.


DATA AND SERVICES

Tombaugh does not redistribute a data set. It retrieves data on request
from public archives and displays it. Please credit the original sources
when you use anything you see in the tool.

SPHEREx
  Level 2 spectral images from the SPHEREx mission, served by the
  NASA/IPAC Infrared Science Archive (IRSA).
  Mission archive DOI: 10.26131/IRSA629 (verify)
  Quick Release 3 DOI: 10.26131/IRSA662 (verify)
  Data Release 1 DOI: [DR1-DOI] (verify)
  https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Missions/spherex.html

  This research has made use of the NASA/IPAC Infrared Science Archive,
  which is funded by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration
  and operated by the California Institute of Technology.

Gaia
  Gaia Data Release 3 source positions, proper motions and parallaxes.
  This work has made use of data from the European Space Agency (ESA)
  mission Gaia (https://www.cosmos.esa.int/gaia), processed by the Gaia
  Data Processing and Analysis Consortium (DPAC,
  https://www.cosmos.esa.int/web/gaia/dpac/consortium). Funding for the
  DPAC has been provided by national institutions, in particular the
  institutions participating in the Gaia Multilateral Agreement.

CDS
  Object names are resolved with the Sesame name resolver and the SIMBAD
  database, operated at CDS, Strasbourg, France.

WiseView
  Tombaugh links to WiseView (http://byw.tools/wiseview) at the same
  coordinates. No WiseView code or data is included.

The full reference list, including the standards and papers the methods
follow, is in docs/SOURCES.md.
